"SciCo: Hierarchical Cross-Document Coreference for Scientific Concepts (https://arxiv.org/html/2104.08809)\nciteturn50view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn48view0\",\"lineno\":185}); Total lines: 388\nL177: We consider a referential hierarchical relation between concepts $x$ and $y$ as an entailment relation between $y$ and $x$ (cite84†Glockner et al., 2018 ).^{6}^{6} 6 Indeed, “John ate an apple” entails “John ate a fruit” because referring to “apple” entails reference to “fruit”.\nL178: We use a model for textual entailment (RoBERTa-large-MNLI) (cite85†Liu et al., 2019 ), representing each cluster $\\mathcal{C}$ by the concatenation of its mentions (without context) [CLS] $\\mathcal{C}_{k}$ </s></s> $\\mathcal{C}_{j}$ </s>, and running RoBERTa-large-MNLI on all $n(n-1)$ cluster pairs $(\\mathcal{C}_{k},\\mathcal{C}_{j})$. The [CLS] embedding is fed into an output layer for entailment classification.\nL179: To avoid creation of cyclical graphs, we adopt a greedy approach adding relations iteratively, starting from the highest entailment scores, discarding relations creating cycles. We apply this model to clusters obtained by each baseline.\nL180: ### 4.2 Unified Model for H-CDCR\nL181: Deciding whether two mentions refer to the same concept or have a hierarchical relation is sometimes non-trivial (e.g POS Tagging $\\xrightarrow{}$ PTB POS Tagging, but artificial neural networks and neural networks refer to the same concept).\nL182: Therefore, we develop a unified model by formulating our task as multiclass classification, where each mention pair $(m_{1},m_{2})$ can be assigned into four classes (0) $m_{1},m_{2}$ corefer (1) $m_{1}$ $\\xrightarrow{}$ $m_{2}$ (2) $m_{2}$ $\\xrightarrow{}$ $m_{1}$ or (3) not related. Consider a topic (pool of candidates,  §cite14†3.2 ), with a set of mentions $\\mathcal{M}$. During training, we learn a pairwise scorer $f(\\cdot,\\cdot)$ by minimizing:\nL183:  | $$L=-\\frac{1}{N}\\sum_{\\begin{subarray}{c}m_{1},m_{2}\\in\\mathcal{M}\\\\\nL184: m_{1}\\neq m_{2}\\end{subarray}}{y\\cdot log(f(m_{1},m_{2}))}$$  |  | (2)\nL185: where $1$-hot $y$ is one of the four classes and $N$ is the number of training pairs. For $f(\\cdot,\\cdot)$ we use Longformer (cite86†Beltagy et al., 2020 ), a transformer-based language model for processing long sequences so that we can encode pairs of full paragraphs. We also use CDLM, a recent variant of Longformer pre-trained for cross-document tasks (cite47†Caciularu et al., 2021 ).\nL186: During pretraining, Longformer and CDLM apply local attention — attention only to tokens in a fixed-sized window around each token. When fine-tuning on a specific task, global attention — attention to all tokens in the sequence — can be assigned to a few target tokens to encode global information. Following cite47†Caciularu et al. (2021) , we take each mention and its corresponding paragraph, inserting mention markers <m> and </m> surrounding the mention to obtain a mention representation.\nL187: For CDLM, we add the document markers <doc-s> and </doc-s> surrounding each document. Then, we concatenate the representations of $m_{1}$ and $m_{2}$ separated by a separator token </s>, and add a [CLS] token at the beginning. We assign global attention to the [CLS] and the mention markers of the two mentions. The [CLS] vector is finally fed into a linear layer $W^{d\\times 4}$, for fine-tuning the model.\nL188: Table 3: Model results. We evaluate strong CDCR baselines, and a unified multiclass model for H-CDCR that outperforms the baselines.\nL189:  | Coreference  | Hierarchy  |  | Path\nL190:  | CoNLL F1  | F1  | F1-50%  |  |  | Ratio\nL191: IAA (AVG)  | 82.7  | 68.9  | 62.8  |  |  | 64.5\nL192: IAA (MAX-Macro)  | 90.2  | 82.3  | 77.7  |  |  | 78.4\nL193: CA_{News}  | 52.4  | 37.1  | 13.0  |  |  | 24.1\nL194: CA_{Sci-News}  | 43.5  | 29.2  | 12.3  |  |  | 21.6\nL195: CA_{SciCo}  | 55.2  | 23.7  | 15.8  |  |  | 21.2\nL196: CA_{SciCo} + CS-RoBERTa  | 57.4  | 23.5  | 16.1  |  |  | 23.6\nL197: CA_{SciCo} + SciBERT  | 66.8  | 23.8  | 17.8  |  |  | 28.4\nL198: Unified_{Longformer}  | 77.2  | 44.5  | 36.1  |  |  | 47.2\nL199: Unified_{CDLM}  | 77.0  | 44.8  | 35.5  |  |  | 45.9\nL200: At inference time, we build clusters using agglomerative clustering over predicted coreference scores, in the same way as in the baseline described above (§cite18†4.1 ). Then, for each pair of clusters $(\\mathcal{C}_{1},\\mathcal{C}_{2})$, we aggregate cross-cluster mention-pair predictions for hierarchical relations as follows.\nL201: Given a pair $(m_{1},m_{2})$ where $m_{1}\\in\\mathcal{C}_{1}$ and $m_{2}\\in\\mathcal{C}_{2}$, we compute the probability score for $m_{1}$ being a child of $m_{2}$, and define the score of $C_{1}$ being the child of $C_{2}$ as the average of all pairwise scores for all $\\{(m_{i},m_{j})|m_{i}\\in\\mathcal{C}_{1},m_{j}\\in\\mathcal{C}_{2}\\}$: $s(\\mathcal{C}_{1},\\mathcal{C}_{2})=\\frac{1}{|\\mathcal{C}_{1}|\\cdot|\\mathcal{C}_{2}|}\\sum_{m_{1}\\in\\mathcal{C}_{1}}\\sum_{m_{2}\\in\\mathcal{C}_{2}}f_{\\text{is-child}}(m_{1},m_{2})$.\nL202: To avoid cycles we apply the same greedy approach as in the baseline models (§cite18†4.1 ) and stop when the hierarchy score is below a tuned threshold. Full implementation details and hyper-parameters are described in Appendix cite39†B.2 .\nL203: ## 5 Experimental Results\nL204: \nL205: Table cite87†3 presents the results of the baseline models as well as our unified solution. Results of coreference are reported using the CoNLL F1 metric. We also report performance with our two metrics introduced earlier (§cite9†2.2 ). See Appendix cite40†C for all coreference metrics, as well as recall and precision for the hierarchy score.\nL206: The results show that training a model to predict both coreference and hierarchy in a multiclass setup outperforms baselines by a large margin across all metrics. In terms of coreference, using the CA baselines (§cite18†4.1 ) trained on external datasests leads to the lowest results. Training the same model on SciCo boosts results, with a significant boost from SciBERT cite83†Beltagy et al. (2019) .\nL207: Focusing on hierarchy, we examine results in terms of our cluster-level hierarchy metric (§cite9†2.2 ) which is designed to not doubly-penalize coreference errors, and the path-based metric. For all baselines, we use a state-of-art pre-trained entailment model cite85†Liu et al. (2019) (see §cite17†4 ) for predicting relations between clusters, which leads to poor results in comparison to the unified model.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSciCo: Hierarchical Cross-Document Coreference for Scientific Concepts (https://arxiv.org/html/2104.08809)\nciteturn50view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn48view0\",\"lineno\":334}); Total lines: 388\nL327: ### A.3 Annotation Interface\nL328: To annotate SciCo, we extend CoRefi [cite74†Bornstein et al., 2020 ] with the ability to annotate hierarchy of clusters.^{10}^{10} 10 cite131†https://github.com/ariecattan/CoRefi†github.com The hierarchy is kept in sync with the clusters – any modification of the clusters (e.g., merging two clusters) affects automatically the hierarchy (e.g., unifying their children). Therefore, annotators can annotate both the clusters and the hierarchy at the same time or alternate between them.\nL329: In addition, annotators can (and are encouraged to) add some notes in the hierarchy to help them distinguish between ambiguous concepts. As shown in Figure cite68†3 , we extend CoRefi by displaying metadata for each paper, including the link to the Semantic Scholar URL of the paper. This additional context is often helpful in order to annotate complex cases.\nL330: For example, as shown in Table cite61†1 , for resolving the ambiguity between two mentions of cross-view training, it may be useful to see that one paper was published a few years before the other one coined its own cross-view training as a name for their semi-supervised model.\nL331: ## B Models\nL332: ### B.1 cite45†Cattan et al. [2021a] ’s CDCR model\nL333: Here, we describe the cross-document resolver of cite45†Cattan et al. [2021a] that we use as baseline for SciCo (§cite18†4.1 ). This model is based on contextualized token and span representations. For a document $d$, we are given a sequence of tokens $\\mathbf{x}=\\{x_{1},x_{2},\\ldots,x_{T}\\}$ where $T$ is the length of the document. We first obtain a contextualized embedding of each $x_{t}\\in\\mathbf{x}$ using the RoBERTa-large pre-trained language model [cite85†Liu et al., 2019 ].\nL334: Each mention span $i$ is a contiguous subsequence of $\\mathbf{x}$, denoted $\\mathbf{x}_{i}=\\{x^{(i)}_{1},x^{(i)}_{2},\\ldots,x^{(i)}_{S}\\}$. Let $\\mathbf{\\hat{x}}_{i}=\\{\\hat{x}^{(i)}_{1},\\hat{x}^{(i)}_{2},\\ldots,\\hat{x}^{(i)}_{S}\\}$ be the sequence of embedded tokens in span $i$, where $\\hat{x}^{(i)}_{t}$ is the embedding of token $t$ in span $i$. Then, similarly to cite132†Lee et al.\nL335: [2017] , each span $i$ is represented (cite133†3 ) by the concatenation of embeddings of the boundary tokens in the span ($\\hat{x}^{(i)}_{1}$ and $\\hat{x}^{(i)}_{S}$), an attention-weighted sum of the token embeddings in span $i$ (Attn$(\\mathbf{\\hat{x}}_{i})$), and a feature vector denoting the span width (number of tokens).\nL336:  | $$g_{i}=[\\hat{x}^{(i)}_{1},\\hat{x}^{(i)}_{S},\\text{Attn}(\\mathbf{\\hat{x}}_{i}),\\phi(i)]$$  |  | (3)\nL337: \nL338: Given a pair of contextualized span embeddings $g_{i}$ and $g_{j}$ from two different documents, we feed them to a pairwise scorer (cite134†4 ) in the form of a simple feed-forward network that receives as input the concatenation of two span representations and their element-wise product, and outputs the likelihood that the two mentions corefer.\nL339:  | $$s(i,j)=\\text{FFNN}([g_{i},g_{j},g_{i}\\circ g_{j}])\\\\\nL340: $$  |  | (4)\nL341: At inference time, the coreference clusters are formed by applying agglomerative clustering cite135†Ward Jr [1963] over a pairwise distance matrix populated with scores $s(i,j)$, using the average-linkage cluster merging criterion. Following [cite45†Cattan et al., 2021a ], this model does not involve fine-tuning of the underlying language model due to prohibitive memory costs — it begins with encoding all documents using an LM, then computes the pairwise scores over all pairs of mention representations.\nL342: We note that our unified approach is the only model we evaluate that fine-tunes the Longformer on our data.\nL343: ### B.2 Hyper parameters for the Unified Model\nL344: We develop our model in Pytorch [cite136†Paszke et al., 2019 ] and PytorchLightning [cite137†Falcon et al., 2019 ] using the Transformers library cite138†Wolf et al. [2020] and the AdamW optimizer. We train our model on 1 epoch using a batch size of 4 and gradient accumulation of 4 and a learning rate of 1E-5. We conduct our experiments on 8 Tesla V100 32GB GPUS using distributed data parallelism. The training time takes about 2.5 hour for the unified model and inference 25 minutes.\nL345: Our model includes 148M parameters. We fine-tune the threshold for the agglomerative clustering and the stopping criterion for the hierarchy on the validation set, in a range of {0.4, 0.6} for both, set to 0.6 and 0.4 respectively. We take the values that achieve the best path ratio metric on the validation set.\nL346: ## C Coreference and Hierarchy Results\nL347: \nL348: Table cite139†6 presents the results of all models according to the cluster-level hierarchy score (§cite9†2.2 ) in terms of recall, precision and F1.\nL349:  | Hierarchy  |  | Hierarchy 50%\nL350: Model  | Recall  | Precision  | F1  |  | Recall  | Precision  | F1\nL351: CA_{News}  | 43.3  | 32.4  | 37.1  |  | 21.8  | 9.3  | 13.0\nL352: CA_{Sci-News}  | 37.8  | 23.7  | 29.2  |  | 12.2  | 12.5  | 12.3\nL353: CA_{SciCo}  | 45.5  | 16.0  | 23.7  |  | 17.7  | 14.3  | 15.8\nL354: CA_{SciCo} + CS-RoBERTa  | 43.6  | 13.1  | 23.5  |  | 19.5  | 13.8  | 16.1\nL355: CA_{SciCo} + SciBERT  | 41.4  | 16.7  | 23.8  |  | 27.2  | 13.3  | 17.8\nL356: Unified_{Longformer}  | 36.6  | 56.7  | 44.5  |  | 29.1  | 48.2  | 36.3\nL357: Unified_{CDLM}  | 36.4  | 58.2  | 44.8  |  | 27.8  | 49.1  | 35.5\nL358: Table 6: Recall, Precision and F1 according to the Cluster-level Hierarchy Score (§cite9†2.2 ) for all models.\nL359: Table cite81†7 presents the results of the inter-annotator agreement (IAA), all baseline models as well as the pipeline and unified, according to all common coreference metrics (MUC, B^{3}, CEAFe, LEA and CoNLL F1). We obtain coreference metrics using the python implementation of the standard coreference metrics [cite79†Moosavi and Strube, 2016 ].^{11}^{11} 11 cite140†https://github.com/ns-moosavi/coval/†github.com Following cite46†Cattan et al.\nL360: [2021b] , we apply coreference metrics only on non-singleton (gold and predicted) clusters in order to avoid inflated results.\nL361:  |  | MUC  |  | $B^{3}$  |  | $CEAFe$  |  | LEA  |  | CoNLL\nL362:  |  | R  | P  | $F_{1}$  |  | R  | P  | $F_{1}$  |  | R  | P  | $F_{1}$  |  | R  | P  | $F_{1}$  |  | $F_{1}$\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://aclanthology.org/2024.acl-long.18.pdf)\nciteturn50view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 21; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn48view1\",\"lineno\":363}); Total lines: 1442\nL320@P3: Full annotation details are in Appendix C.\nL321@P3: 5Training and hyperparameter details in Appendix B.\nL322@P3: 3.1 Inspiration Retrieval Module\nL323@P3: We take broad inspiration from cognitive aspects of\nL324@P3: innovation (Hope et al., 2023): when researchers\nL325@P3: generate a new idea, they are grounded in a web\nL326@P3: of existing concepts and papers bearing on the new\nL327@P3: idea. We aim to enrich the context of each back\u0002ground by retrieving “inspirations”— pieces of in\u0002formation that can guide hypothesis generation. As\nL328@P3: illustrated in Figure 2, for a given instance of the\nL329@P3: SCIMON task, our retrieval augmentation can re\u0002trieve from three types of sources. Each source\nL330@P3: uses a different form of query and output.\nL331@P3: Semantic Neighbors For a given prob\u0002lem/motivation as input, ideas proposed for related\nL332@P3: problems in the training set can serve as a guiding\nL333@P3: reference for generating a new idea. Given the\nL334@P3: background context B with a seed term v and\nL335@P3: problem/motivation M, we construct a base\nL336@P3: input b: a concatenation of M with a prompt P\nL337@P3: belonging to one of two templates: “v is used\nL338@P3: for p” or “v is done by using p”, where p is one\nL339@P3: of Task/Method/Material/Metric. In short, b :=\nL340@P3: P ⊕ context:M. For example, in Figure 2, the\nL341@P3: concatenation is “Knowledge acquisition is done\nL342@P3: by using Method; Context:...requires plms to\nL343@P3: integrate information...lifelong manner...”.\nL344@P3: We then retrieve inputs from the training set that\nL345@P3: are semantically related to a new base input b, and\nL346@P3: obtain target sentences T corresponding to each\nL347@P3: retrieved training input. We extract the target term\nL348@P3: t ∈ T matching the seed term in b (§2.2) as in\u0002spiration for input b. Simply put, this means we\nL349@P3: use as inspiration the salient aspect of the solution\nL350@P3: proposed in T , which we found empirically to help\nL351@P3: remove noisy/irrelevant information in T . For ex\u0002ample, in Figure 2, we find “informative entities\nL352@P3: are done by using Method context: in this work, we\nL353@P3: aim at equipping pre-trained language models with\nL354@P3: structured knowledge.” as similar to the input and\nL355@P3: use t =“linked knowledge graph” as inspiration.\nL356@P3: Technically, we first construct a fully connected\nL357@P3: graph GS based on the training set where each node\nL358@P3: is a pair of input text bi and target term ti. We\nL359@P3: define the weight between two nodes i and j as\nL360@P3: the cosine similarity between bi and bj based on\nL361@P3: representations from SentenceBERT (Reimers and\nL362@P3: Gurevych, 2019) (all-mpnet-base-v2). Given b,\nL363@P3: we first insert it into GS and compute the weights\nL364@P3: of its connected edges. We then retrieve neighbors\nL365@P3: input text {b1, . . . , bk} from the training set with\nL366@P3: the largest edge weight, where k is the number of\nL367@P3-4: 282retrieved instances. We consider the corresponding\nL368@P4: target terms {t1, . . . , tk} as semantic inspirations.\nL369@P4: KG Neighbors We also explore enriching the\nL370@P4: context by linking it to a background KG with in\u0002formation on related methods and tasks. Using the\nL371@P4: same IE process used to extract our training exam\u0002ples (§2.2), we create a global background KG GB\nL372@P4: which covers all papers in the corpus DY prior to a\nL373@P4: given year Y (i.e., the nodes in GB correspond to\nL374@P4: tasks/methods/materials/metrics, and the edges are\nL375@P4: used-for relations, extracted and normalized from\nL376@P4: across the entire corpus as described earlier). Then,\nL377@P4: given a seed term v at query time, we select ad\u0002jacent nodes {n1, n2, ...} from GB as inspirations.\nL378@P4: As an example, in Figure 2, the neighbor nodes of\nL379@P4: “knowledge acquisition” include “collaborative web\nL380@P4: text annotation editor”, “image matching”, etc.,\nL381@P4: which we select as inspirations.\nL382@P4: Citation Neighbors Another notion of contex\u0002tual relatedness we explore is via citation graph\nL383@P4: links. Here, given as input background context B,\nL384@P4: we assume access to the original source document\nL385@P4: d from which B was extracted, and consider its\nL386@P4: cited paper title set Cd as potential candidates. This\nL387@P4: can be seen as a stronger assumption on informa\u0002tion available to the model— assuming a researcher\nL388@P4: using the model provides relevant candidate docu\u0002ments from which ideas could be pooled. Because\nL389@P4: the training set only contains papers before year\nL390@P4: Y, we only select papers CdY ⊆ Cd prior to year Y.\nL391@P4: We then retrieve the top-k titles with the highest\nL392@P4: cosine similarity to d from CdY based on their Sen\u0002tenceBERT embeddings as earlier. For instance,\nL393@P4: in Figure 2, the paper ELLE (Qin et al., 2022)\nL394@P4: cites the paper (de Masson d'Autume et al., 2019).\nL395@P4: Therefore, we choose the title “episodic memory\nL396@P4: in lifelong language learning” as inspiration infor\u0002mation.\nL397@P4: 3.2 Generation Module\nL398@P4: The idea generation module is given retrieved inspi\u0002rations i1, . . . , ik along with context M as input.\nL399@P4: In-Context Learning We experiment\nL400@P4: with recent state-of-the-art LLMs, GPT3.5\nL401@P4: davinci-003 (Ouyang et al., 2022) and GPT4\nL402@P4: gpt-4-0314 checkpoint (OpenAI, 2023). We first\nL403@P4: ask the model to generate sentences based on the\nL404@P4: seed term and the context in the zero-shot setting\nL405@P4: without any in-context examples (GPT3.5ZS,\nL406@P4: GPT4ZS). We then ask the model to generate\nL407@P4: sentences in a few-shot setting by prompting\nL408@P4: randomly chosen pairs of input and output from the\nL409@P4: training set (GPT3.5FS, GPT4FS). Inspired by Liu\nL410@P4: et al. (2022), we further employ a few-shot setting\nL411@P4: using semantically similar examples. Instead of\nL412@P4: random in-context examples, we use the top-k\nL413@P4: examples from the training set with the highest\nL414@P4: cosine similarity to the query (GPT3.5Retr). This\nL415@P4: few-shot retrieval setting differs from the semantic\nL416@P4: neighbor discussed above, in that we provide both\nL417@P4: the input and output of each instance rather than\nL418@P4: solely supplying target entities as additional input.\nL419@P4: Fine Tuning We fine-tune T5 (Raffel et al., 2020)\nL420@P4: (more recent models may be used too; see our\nL421@P4: biomedical experiment §4.1 fine-tuning an LLM).\nL422@P4: We observe that the generation models tend to copy\nL423@P4: phrases from the background context. For example,\nL424@P4: given the context “...hierarchical tables challenge\nL425@P4: numerical reasoning ...”, the model will generate\nL426@P4: “hierarchical table reasoning for question answering”\nL427@P4: as the top prediction. For generating suggestions of\nL428@P4: novel ideas, we wish to discourage overly copying\nL429@P4: from the background context. We introduce a new\nL430@P4: in-context contrastive objective, where negative ex\u0002amples are taken from the text in the input (e.g.,\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSOLVENT (https://www.hyadatalab.com/papers/analogy-cscw18.pdf)\nciteturn50view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 21; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn48view2\",\"lineno\":285}); Total lines: 904\nL261@P6: A Comparison of Social, Learning, and Finan\u0002cial Strategies on Crowd Engagement and Out\u0002put Quality\nL262@P6: One main challenge in large creative online communities is\nL263@P6: helping their members find inspirational ideas from a large\nL264@P6: pool of ideas. A high-level approach to address this chal\u0002lenge is to create a synthesis of emerging solution space that\nL265@P6: can be used to provide participants with creative and diverse\nL266@P6: inspirational ideas of others. Existing approaches to gener\u0002ate the synthesis of solution space either require community\nL267@P6: members to engage in tasks that detract from the main activ\u0002ity of generating ideas or depend on external crowd workers\nL268@P6: to help organize the ideas. We built IDEAHOUND a collab\u0002orative idea generation system that demonstrates an alter\u0002native ”organic” human computation approach, where com\u0002munity members (rather than external crowds) contribute\nL269@P6: feedback about ideas as a byproduct of an activity that nat\u0002urally integrates into the ideation process. This feedback\nL270@P6: in turn helps the community identify diverse inspirational\nL271@P6: ideas that can prompt community members to generate\nL272@P6: more high-quality and diverse ideas.\nL273@P6: A significant challenge for crowdsourcing has been increas\u0002ing worker engagement and output quality. We explore\nL274@P6: the effects of social, learning, and financial strategies, and\nL275@P6: their combinations, on increasing worker retention across\nL276@P6: tasks and change in the quality of worker output. Through\nL277@P6: three experiments, we show that 1) using these strategies\nL278@P6: together increased workers’ engagement and the quality of\nL279@P6: their work; 2) a socialstrategy was most effective for increas\u0002ing engagement; 3) a learning strategy was most effective in\nL280@P6: improving quality. The findings of this paper provide strate\u0002gies for harnessing the crowd to perform complex tasks, as\nL281@P6: well as insight into crowd workers’ motivation.\nL282@P6: Top 10 nearest words for\nL283@P6: Abstract: ideas idea analogies proposals productively sub\u0002strates fictions discoveries brainstorm schemas\nL284@P6: Abstract: efficient expressive retention responsive suscep\u0002tibility differentially enjoyment disengagement alertness\nL285@P6: Background: ideas creative innovative inspirational analo\u0002gies productively fictions brainstorm substrates wishingBackground: crowd workers worker shepherding sourcing\nL286@P6: harnessing intrinsically legitimacy rubrics extrinsic\nL287@P6: Purpose: curate interleave scaffold organise devise stimu\u0002late formulate ideas consolidating mobilizePurpose: retention differentially worker soundness media\u0002tors maximize susceptibility redefining greatly decreasing\nL288@P6: Mechanism: idea ideas ideation endeavor grassroots real\u0002ization productively scaffold generative prospectorMechanism: nine eleven twelve fourteen fieldwork com\u0002prehensively separately uncompensated sixteen thirteen\nL289@P6: Findings: ideas community cultivate cohesive heteroge\u0002neous divergent productively repertoire mobilize socializeFindings: tactic efficient disengagement responsive playful\nL290@P6: attentiveness partnerships paradoxically expressive\nL291@P6: Table 1. Examples of modified annotation scheme applied to a system-oriented paper (top left) and an\nL292@P6: “understanding”-oriented paper (top right), and top 10 nearest words for all abstract words VS. each annotation\nL293@P6: vector for each paper (bottom)\nL294@P6: term and document frequencies3. This is a conceptually similar approach to that taken by Hope et\nL295@P6: al [22] and helps us tease apart words that are important for denoting the purpose/mechanism/etc.\nL296@P6: of documents, as opposed to generalized importance. The end result is that each document is\nL297@P6: represented by 4 annotation-specific semantic vectors (background, purpose, mechanism, and\nL298@P6: findings), which we denote as bi ∈ R\nL299@P6: D , pi ∈ RD , mi ∈ RD , and fi ∈ RD , respectively (where D is\nL300@P6: the dimensionality of the base word vectors we use to represent the words).\nL301@P6: To give an intuition of what the vectors mean, Table 1 (bottom) shows the top 10 nearest neighbor\nL302@P6: words for each annotation type for two example papers, as well as for a vector representing the full\nL303@P6: abstract (i.e., TF-IDF-weighted average of all non-stopwords in the abstract). The nearest neighbors\nL304@P6: 3This step is implemented in compute-tf-idfs.py in the code provided in the supplementary material\nL305@P6-7: Proceedings of the ACM on Human-Computer Interaction, Vol. 2, No. CSCW, Article 31. Publication date: November 2018.31:8 J. Chan et al.\nL306@P7: are quite different across the different annotation types and from the full abstract vector, and also\nL307@P7: map well to the meaning of those annotation types. For example, while the overall abstract for\nL308@P7: the first example is about improving creativity in online communities (as seen in the top words\nL309@P7: for the abstract vector, {ideas, idea, analogies, proposals...}), the core purpose of enabling efficient\nL310@P7: synthesis of the ideas generated by the community is well captured by the words {curate, interleave,\nL311@P7: scaffold, organise, devise...}, and the mechanism of “piggy-backing” on ideation is captured by the\nL312@P7: mechanism top words, {idea, ideas, ideation, endeavor, grassroots...}. The term “grassroots” in\nL313@P7: particular is interesting because it captures the idea of sustainability, which is completely missed in\nL314@P7: the top words for the abstract and background vectors. In contrast, for the second, understanding\u0002oriented paper, the mechanism top words are very research-methods oriented, {nine, eleven, twelve,\nL315@P7: fourteen, fieldwork...}.\nL316@P7: 3.3 Analogical similarity metrics\nL317@P7: Using these specialized vectors, solvent can support a number of interesting analogical queries:\nL318@P7: (1) purpose+mechanism : find other research papers with a similar purpose AND mechanism, by\nL319@P7: computing the average of the cosine between their purpose vectors and the cosine betwen\nL320@P7: their mechanism vectors, i.e., ⟨cos(p1, p2), cos(m1, m2)⟩ for any pair of paper1 and paper2. By\nL321@P7: ignoring background/implications, we aim to achieve some level of abstraction away from\nL322@P7: the domain context.\nL323@P7: (2) purpose : find other research papers with a similar purpose, but not necessarily a similar\nL324@P7: mechanism, with cos(m1, m2) This should find alternative mechanisms for solving some\nL325@P7: target problem.\n"
